+def robust_fit_articulated_pose_from_anchors(
+    source_points_o: np.ndarray,
+    target_points_w: np.ndarray,
+    weights: np.ndarray,
+    part_ids: np.ndarray,
+    axis_o: np.ndarray,
+    origin_o: np.ndarray,
+    *,
+    inlier_threshold_mm: float,
+    min_anchors: int = 3,
+    min_moving_anchors: int = 3,
+    external_unit_scale_to_meter: float = 1.0,
+    theta_init: float = 0.0,
+    theta_reference_rad: float | None = None,
+    theta_limits_rad: tuple[float, float] | None = None,
+    theta_residual_ratio: float = 3.0,
+    joint_type: str = "revolute",
+    joint_limits: tuple[float, float] | None = None,
+) -> dict[str, Any]:
+    """Articulated counterpart of ``robust_fit_pose_from_anchors``.
+
+    Same shape of contract -- fit, threshold on residual, refit on the inliers --
+    so the caller only has to supply the part labels and the joint.
+
+    Two failure modes have no analogue in the rigid version and are reported rather
+    than papered over. Too few anchors on the moving part leaves the angle
+    unobserved, and the honest answer is the previous angle with the body fitted
+    around it, flagged ``theta_unobserved``; averaging in a guess would look like a
+    measurement. And ``theta_limits_rad`` clamps only at the end, because a clamped
+    angle is no longer the least-squares solution -- when it binds, the estimate is
+    censored and ``theta_clamped`` says so. On this object the scanned joint range
+    proved about 9 deg short of the real hinge, so a limit is as likely to be wrong
+    as the estimate it truncates.
+
+    The third is the angle running away. Nothing in triangulation knows the lid
+    cannot pass through the table, so when the flow loses a lid lying flat -- the
+    pose where turning either way looks much the same -- the angle can walk off and
+    settle somewhere impossible, with a full complement of moving anchors the whole
+    time. Counting anchors cannot catch that: they were all present, just wrong.
+    What does catch it is that a wrong angle can no longer explain where
+    triangulation put the moving anchors while the static ones stay explained, so
+    the two parts' residuals come apart. Judging the moving part against the static
+    part rather than against a millimetre threshold keeps this free of a constant
+    that would need retuning per object, rig, or unit system: it asks whether this
+    frame's moving anchors fit worse than its own static ones, and both share
+    whatever noise the frame has.
+
+    When it fires the angle is held and the body is fitted from the static anchors
+    alone. Leaving the moving anchors in would let a broken angle drag the body pose
+    with it through the shared Kabsch -- smoothly and with no jump, which is exactly
+    how it hid on the capture that motivated this.
