+    ``joint_type`` selects what the joint coordinate means, and the limits follow it
+    into separate arguments on purpose. ``theta_limits_rad`` is radians and belongs to
+    a revolute joint; ``joint_limits`` is in the joint's own unit -- radians for a
+    hinge, the anchors' external length unit for a slide -- and is the one to use for
+    anything prismatic. Passing radians to a sliding joint is the most damaging
+    mistake available here, because a displacement read as an angle raises nothing and
+    tracks a joint that is off by orders of magnitude, so the two are kept apart and
+    mixing them is an error rather than a coercion.
+
+    A prismatic result reports ``joint_value`` and omits ``theta_rad``/``theta_deg``
+    entirely. Emitting ``degrees(displacement)`` would be a number, and a consumer
+    plotting it would get a plausible curve of a quantity that does not exist; a
+    missing key stops that consumer instead.
